      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Gout is a common rheumatologic condition of recurrent polyarticular involvement. Tophi may be the first sign of the disease. We report a 55-year-old hypertensive, chronic alcoholic and diabetic male with a three-year history of multiple painful skin-colored nodules on the forearms, elbows, feet, posterior thighs, and popliteal fossa associated with discharging sinuses. Serum uric acid was 8.1 mg/dL (normal: 2-7.4 mg/dL). Fine needle aspiration cytology showed monosodium urate crystals, which are pathognomonic for gout, confirmed histopathalogically."},
